// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TEMPLATE — personal Claude Code settings for this project.
//
// To use it:
//     cp .claude/settings.local.json.example .claude/settings.local.json
// (PowerShell: Copy-Item .claude\settings.local.json.example .claude\settings.local.json)
//
// Then DELETE EVERY LINE STARTING WITH "//". Settings are plain JSON and do not
// allow comments — a file with them is silently ignored, which looks exactly like
// having no settings at all.
//
// The copy is gitignored on purpose: it holds what is true of your machine, not
// of the project. The shared baseline everyone gets is .claude/settings.json —
// put anything the whole team needs there instead, so it travels with the repo.
//
// The two files merge (project first, then yours); yours adds to the baseline
// rather than replacing it. Empty is a perfectly good starting point — add a rule
// when a prompt for the same command annoys you a second time.
//
// What belongs here:
//   - allowances for tools you personally use (web search, a local formatter);
//   - shell commands with paths that exist only on your machine;
//   - directories outside the repository you want readable.
// What does not:
//   - one-off commands from a single conversation — they age into noise;
//   - anything a teammate would also need — that belongs in settings.json;
//   - secrets or tokens of any kind. This file is not encrypted, and one
//     mistaken "git add -f" publishes it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": [
      // "WebSearch",
      // "Bash(py -m pytest *)",
      // "Read(//c/some/other/directory/**)"
    ]
  }
}
